I've dealt with the other thing before.

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:21.720
So now I got to put them together.

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:25.260
And it does up the challenge a little bit.

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:31.540
But coming off of Battle of Olympus, that was a very story-driven game and had a large sense of adventure.

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:35.180
Forget that this game could be short if you're perfect, right?

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:37.380
Like, obviously, like, this was not a short game for me.

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:41.220
I wasn't even able to beat it, but it was rather difficult for me.

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:45.960
The sense of, like, that you're doing anything even.

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:47.660
Like, does it feel...

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:51.300
Obviously, like, shmups don't necessarily feel like that in general anyway,

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:52.460
that they're telling some kind of story.

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:57.520
But it's interesting that at the end of the game, you're greeted by, like, this woman who says,

00:18:57.840 --> 00:18:59.140
well done, Cybernoid Pilot.

00:18:59.140 --> 00:19:01.100
You have defeated the Baycores.

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:06.740
Until the next time, it's like, does it feel like you've actually done anything to justify...

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:08.640
Like, for the most part, you're just maneuvering around.

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:11.820
If anything, it feels like I've escaped the Baycores.

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:13.720
Yeah, there's also no...

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:20.180
Like, they have not earned the weird character design that they gave this screen.

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:22.900
I don't know who that's supposed to be.

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:29.320
Because the only other reference to any story is just in the manual.

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:32.940
So, otherwise, this is just a puzzle game.

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:37.800
So, it feels entirely unearned.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:41.230
Yeah, I feel like it gives you the vibe of, like...

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:43.620
The vibe of escaping.

00:19:43.820 --> 00:19:46.620
Like you said, it gives me almost like a Metroid feel.

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:49.860
Where, like, oh, I'm in this hostile, scary environment.

00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:51.220
I feel like they could have gone with that.

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:52.780
And it would have felt a little more earned.

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:55.840
Just because that's what the tone says about it.

00:19:55.900 --> 00:19:56.840
Even without any story.

00:19:57.080 --> 00:19:59.580
But then to try and kind of insert this other, like...

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:01.380
Feeling like that there was this other story going on.

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:02.460
Yeah, felt a little off.

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:08.160
Yeah, I mean, the coolest part presentationally about the game is the starting screen.

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:10.560
With your ship.

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:13.700
And showing you, like, all the tools you have.

00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:15.160
And the blueprints of it.

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:18.880
And it has that classified and a border and everything like that.

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:19.980
Doesn't mean anything.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:23.060
It's not like you can choose a loadout or something.

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:25.980
But that's the cool screen to me.

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:27.520
And that's great because it's screen one.

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:30.420
So it kind of makes you excited to be this ship.

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:35.320
And then I guess I just wasn't expecting what came next.

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:40.580
Based on just how I've been trained to play NES games over the last 200 episodes.

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:43.200
This was surprising, this kind of game.

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:48.080
Should they have went, like, more extreme in either direction?

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:52.260
Like, should this have been a little more open and shmup-like?

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:58.440
Or maybe forget all of the shooting and just make it a defensive puzzle-solving game in a spaceship?

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:01.300
I don't know. They didn't do that, so it's hard to say.

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:09.380
But I feel like this mix of three genres, really, is the confusing part of the game for me.

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:16.300
I think if this game had a story that wasn't just the end screen.

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:19.280
And there were missions.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:20.740
This was split up in missions.

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:26.760
And there was a little bit of some context behind each mission.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:36.060
And maybe it wasn't just the same weird pink meat moss as the background of each.

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:42.500
And maybe that that weirdly designed lady was giving you, like, some story beats.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:47.420
I think that, like, I could have done more with this.

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:51.280
Like, I could have actually been engaged more.

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:54.960
But as it stands now, like, no.

00:21:55.500 --> 00:22:01.560
See, I don't know that I would need, like, more, like, story, like, in that sense, quote-unquote.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:09.540
So, but for me, it feels like there's something really cool here about the way that this game, like, about this type of gameplay.

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:12.840
That I feel like we haven't really seen this type of thing so much before.

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:26.240
I just, I would want them to, like, I don't know, more intentionally design the progress, you know?

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:33.480
I want to learn about a thing and then feel like it's a little more complex and then, like, learn about a new thing, you know?

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:43.200
I feel like it's kind of the randomness that throws me off a little bit where it's, like, if it felt like it was, like, teaching me as I'm going.

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:49.880
And, like, it felt like, okay, this is really progressing consistently in Fire of Difficulty.

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:57.340
I think that would help it, like, I don't even, I could be engaged in something like this because I'm always, like, well, what's the next challenge going to be?

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:04.840
I mean, obviously, I like a good story, too, but, like, I just think that the idea of this type of game is cool in theory.

00:23:05.020 --> 00:23:07.820
So I think that you can, like, keep people engaged without it.

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:12.280
You know how we joke about Victor Kai being just one guy?

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:14.580
Yeah. Like, just Victor?

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:17.080
This game really was one guy.

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:24.180
Why? Raphael Checo programmed the entire game, just not the NES version.

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:25.820
Oh. Yeah.

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:36.740
He programmed the original computer version and then Acclaim brought it on to the NES and hired Studio 12 Productions to handle the port of it.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:44.060
And I guess, like, you know, update the graphics, obviously, to be much improved from Raphael's original prototype.

00:23:45.240 --> 00:23:52.160
But interesting that the game is, like, yeah, well, I guess, like, you know, Raphael must have signed off on it, right?

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:54.600
Being like, cool, like, you're going to pay me how much money for my game?

00:23:54.660 --> 00:24:01.640
Like, go for it. You know, probably didn't want to then, like, learn assembly and figure out how to make it on the NES.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:08.500
Just funny that this is one of those examples of a game that was a one-man band back then.

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:15.400
Like, it's interesting now when we still have indie games that are like, you know, Stardew Valley, where it's like one guy just doing it all.

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:17.000
It's like, that doesn't seem possible.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:23.120
Oh, I think back then, too, it was like, oh, the guy made Stardew Valley and also Unity.

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:25.260
So, I mean... Right, right, right.

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:26.280
I guess that's what I'm saying.

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:39.660
It's like, yeah, it's also, like, even though it's much less impressive of a game by today's standards, it's pretty impressive that one guy managed to program a game that became such a big hit on computers that a claim was like, yeah, we want this on the NES.

00:24:40.260 --> 00:24:44.480
Yeah, it definitely makes it a lot more impressive knowing that one guy made this, like...

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:46.540
Yeah. Good job, Raphael.

00:24:46.700 --> 00:24:50.660
Yeah. No disrespect with my negative comments, Roth.

00:24:50.940 --> 00:24:52.640
Yeah, I guess...

00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:58.200
As I call him by, like, a nickname he didn't ask me to call him by.

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:10.460
Yeah. To Sean's point, though, what does it feel like when you program an entire game by yourself for the ZX Spectrum and then they bring in an entire team of people to put it on the NES?

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:11.400
Like, that must feel...

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:21.480
We got that, yeah. Yeah, but that must feel, like, pretty good, though, too, that, you know, you were like, I did this all by myself and now you need an entire team and a publisher and some marketers.

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:23.320
Like, you guys need all this.

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:24.920
I was able to do this on my own.

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:34.200
Or maybe it was more insidious and he had sold them the rights, but he made a little thing in the contract that was like, but I'm not going to help you port it.

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:41.140
And then he was laughing at them very rudely as he left the office and they were just like, what are we going to do?

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:43.520
Like, we got to hire somebody.

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:44.900
And that's how the game was made.

00:25:44.900 --> 00:25:52.880
Yeah, they hired, like, a ragtag team of, like, the world's most, like, scrappy developers and they had to learn to get along.

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:54.300
They all smoke.

00:25:55.120 --> 00:25:57.880
From that point on, he was known as Cyberknock.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:10.200
Lockrider. Lately, we haven't had many sequels and spinoffs, but this one has a spinoff sequel, rather.

00:26:10.420 --> 00:26:11.240
Not a spinoff, sorry.

00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:15.700
I screwed up the one thing that maybe I could have gotten right, but I didn't do it.

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:20.000
It is Cybernoid 2, The Revenge.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:26.880
Was enough damage done in this game to, yeah, was enough damage done to justify any revenge?

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:32.920
Is it, like, all of these enemies flying through you as a ship now and all your obstacles?

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:35.840
Right, they're, like, bacteria.

00:26:36.260 --> 00:26:37.680
Wow, that sounds fun.

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:40.440
It's the exact same game with updated graphics.

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:49.420
Oh. I mean, like, when I say the exact same game, I don't mean that it's the same game, like, with updated graphics.

00:26:49.420 --> 00:26:51.180
I mean, it's the same kind of gameplay.

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:56.360
Yeah. So you don't mean it's the same game when you say it's the same game.

00:26:57.480 --> 00:26:59.260
Right. I meant it's the same gameplay.

00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:03.100
Yeah, they haven't changed anything else.

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:05.600
New environments.

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:09.100
Cybernoid 2, The Revenge.

00:27:09.480 --> 00:27:11.700
Are they revenging Joe's brother?

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:12.860
About time.

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:14.280
I played that game, by the way.

00:27:14.380 --> 00:27:15.580
I should talk about it one day.

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:17.960
Not now, though, please.

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:19.760
Yeah. Not now.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:25.720
Also, interesting that this points out that Cybernoid, The Fighting Machine, is not, like,

00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:30.080
that was the subtitle, you know, The Fighting Machine.

00:27:30.080 --> 00:27:34.540
And now it's not, like, Cybernoid, The Fighting Machine 2, The Revenge.

00:27:34.540 --> 00:27:37.640
It's just, like, Cybernoid is the title.

00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:40.540
That is the, you are the Cybernoid ship.

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:44.180
And this one was called The Fighting Machine.

00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:49.440
All right. But before we move on to the Essential Games list, can I just pose a question?

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:52.380
You may not know the answer, but you've got some info.

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:53.820
So maybe you do. Yeah, go for it.

00:27:54.380 --> 00:28:01.760
Did they just neglect to remove the, like, was this like an arcade thing where there were credits?

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:04.060
Did you ever get extra credits?

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:12.140
Or is it just, like, a vestige of an earlier version of the game that it's even referencing credits?

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:16.360
Why is there a thing that says that you don't get additional credits?

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:20.300
Great question. Don't know the answer.

00:28:20.500 --> 00:28:22.380
Great. Maybe it's just to... Oh, that's a question for the audience.

00:28:22.640 --> 00:28:25.600
It's just to make you realize, like, it's to make you feel the hopelessness of this game.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:29.040
Like, oh, you still didn't really, uh, get it, do that well.

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:31.360
There's no way you can.

